 Note that $a_0 \neq 0,$ so we may rewrite this as $a_0 a_1 a_2 \dotsm = \frac{1}{\frac{1}{a_0} \cdot \frac{1}{a_1} \cdot \frac{1}{a_2} \dotsm}.$  Each $\frac{1}{a_n}$ satisfies the recursion $\frac{1}{a_n} = (1 + \frac{1}{a_n -1})^2,$ so if we can find an $n$ with $\frac{1}{a_n - 1} = 0,$ then we are done.  But if $\frac{1}{a_n} = 0,$ then $\frac{1}{a_0} \cdot \frac{1}{a_1} \dotsm = 0.$
As for how to choose $n$ so that this happens, we notice that if $a_{n - 1} = 1,$ then $\frac{1}{a_{n- 1} - 1} = 0,$ which means we can take $n = 1.$

To show that $a_{n - 1} =1,$ we can rewrite the recurrence as
\[\frac{1}{a_n} = \frac{a_{n - 1} a_{n - 2}}{a_{n - 2} + a_{n - 1}}.\]So if $\frac{1}{a_n} = \frac{a_{n - 1}}{a_{n - 2} + a_{n - 1}}$ and $\frac{1}{a_{n - 1}} = \frac{a_{n - 2}}{a_{n - 2} + a_{n - 1}},$ then
\[\frac{1}{a_n} = \frac{1}{a_{n - 1} + a_{n - 2}}.\]Writing this as a determinant, we have that
\[\begin{vmatrix}
1 & a_{n - 2} & a_{n - 2} + a_{n - 1} \\
1 & a_{n - 1} & a_{n - 1} + a_{n - 2} \\
1 & a_{n} & a_{n} + a_{n -1}
\end{vmatrix} = 0.\]Expanding across the third row gives
\[\begin{vmatrix}
1 & a_{n - 2} \\
a_{n - 1} & a_{n - 1} + a_{n - 2}
\end{vmatrix}
\times\begin{vmatrix}
1 & a_{n - 2} \\
1 & a_{n}
\end{vmatrix} - \begin{vmatrix}
a_{n - 2} & a_{n - 2} + a_{n - 1} \\
1 & a_{n - 1}
\end{vmatrix} \times\begin{vmatrix}
1 & a_{n - 2} \\
1 & a_{n -1} + a_{n}
\end{vmatrix}.\]We see that the first determinant is
\[a_{n - 1} - a_{n - 2},\]while the second determinant will not be helpful, as it is equal to
\[\begin{vmatrix}
a_{n - 2} + a_{n - 1} & 1 \\
a_{n -2}& 1
\end{vmatrix} =
a_{n - 1} - a_{n - 2}.\]Therefore, $(a_{n - 1} - a_{n - 2})^2 = 0,$ and we conclude that $a_{n - 1} = a_{n - 2}.$ As the sequence is defined by recursion, this proves that $a_{n - 1} = 1,$ and $\frac{1}{a_n - 1} = 0.$ Therefore,
\[a_0 a_1 a_2 \dotsm = 1.\] </answer>

